  "estimatedMinutes": 2,
  "statement": "If \\(m,\\) \\(n,\\) \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are real numbers and \\(f(x)=mx+n\\) and \\(g(x)=px+q,\\) then the equation \\(f(g(x))=g(f(x))\\) has a solution",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "for all choices of \\(m,\\) \\(n,\\) \\(p\\) and \\(q\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "if and only if \\(m=p\\) and \\(n=q\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "if and only if \\(mq-np=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "if and only if \\(n(1-p)-q(1-m)=0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "if and only if \\((1-n)(1-p)\\) \\({}-(1-q)(1-m)=0\\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "d",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1976-p10.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "We have \\[ \\begin{aligned} f(g(x))&=mpx+mq+n,\\\\ g(f(x))&=pmx+pn+q. \\end{aligned} \\] The \\(x\\)-terms cancel for every \\(x,\\) so a solution exists exactly when \\(mq+n=pn+q.\\) Rearranging gives \\(n(1-p)-q(1-m)=0.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.",
      "hints": [
        "Expand both compositions before trying to solve for \\(x\\)",
        "The coefficients of \\(x\\) are automatically equal, leaving a condition on the constant terms"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
